package com.nulljosh.nimble

/**
 * BC 20th Century World History 12 with no network: the world wars and the peace, the Russian and Chinese
 * revolutions, authoritarian states, the Second World War and the Holocaust, the Cold War, decolonization, human
 * rights and genocide. A port of `hist12()` in docs/engine.js; the tables are copied from there by script.
 */
internal object WorldHistory12 {
    /** Phrase questions, matched anywhere. */
    private val facts = listOf(
        """\b(?:when|what year) did the berlin wall (?:fall|come down|open)\b|\bwhen was the berlin wall (?:torn down|opened|destroyed)\b""" to "November 9, 1989, when East Germany opened its border crossings and crowds began tearing the wall down. Germany reunified on October 3, 1990.",
        """\bwhen did india (?:gain|get|win|become) (?:its )?independen""" to "August 15, 1947, when British rule ended and the subcontinent was partitioned into India and Pakistan.",
        """\b(?:main |long[- ]term |underlying )?causes? of (?:the )?(?:world war (?:1|one|i)|first world war|great war|ww1|wwi)\b""" to "Historians sum them up as MAIN: militarism (an arms race, above all Britain and Germany's navies), alliances (the Triple Entente against the Triple Alliance), imperialism (rivalry for colonies) and nationalism (in the Balkans above all). The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on June 28, 1914.",
        """\b(?:when|what year) did (?:the )?(?:soviet union|ussr) (?:collapse|fall|end|break up|dissolve)\b|\b(?:collapse|fall|end|breakup|dissolution) of the (?:soviet union|ussr)\b""" to "December 1991. Gorbachev resigned on December 25, 1991 and the Soviet Union broke into 15 independent republics, ending the Cold War.",
        """\b(?:when|what year) did (?:the )?russian revolution\b|\bwhen was (?:the )?russian revolution\b""" to "1917: the February Revolution ended the tsar's rule in March 1917 (February in the old calendar), and the Bolsheviks seized power in the October Revolution (November 1917).",
    ).map { Regex(it.first) to it.second }

    /** People, events and ideas, matched as the whole term by Terms. */
    private val terms = listOf(
        """(?:the )?assassination of (?:archduke )?franz ferdinand|(?:archduke )?franz ferdinand(?:'s)? assassination|(?:archduke )?franz ferdinand""" to "The heir to the throne of Austria-Hungary. His assassination in Sarajevo on June 28, 1914 by Gavrilo Princip, a Bosnian Serb nationalist, set off the chain of declarations that began the First World War.",
        """(?:the )?schlieffen plan""" to "Germany's plan to avoid a long two-front war: knock France out fast by sweeping through neutral Belgium, then turn east to fight Russia. It stalled at the Marne in 1914, and the invasion of Belgium brought Britain into the war.",
        """(?:woodrow )?wilson'?s fourteen points|(?:the )?fourteen points|(?:the )?14 points""" to "US President Woodrow Wilson's 1918 plan for peace: open diplomacy, freedom of the seas, free trade, arms reduction, self-determination for the peoples of Europe's empires, and a League of Nations to keep the peace. Few points survived the Treaty of Versailles.",
        """(?:the )?triple entente""" to "The alliance of Britain, France and Russia before and during the First World War, built from treaties in 1894, 1904 and 1907 to balance Germany.",
        """(?:the )?triple alliance""" to "The 1882 alliance of Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy. When war came in 1914 Italy stayed out, then joined the other side in 1915.",
        """(?:the )?(?:first )?battle of the somme""" to "The British and French offensive on the Western Front from July to November 1916. Britain lost about 57,000 men on the first day alone, over a million soldiers on all sides were killed or wounded, and the line moved only about 10 km. Newfoundland's regiment was nearly wiped out at Beaumont-Hamel.",
        """total war""" to "A war in which a country throws its whole society into the fight: the economy is turned to war production, civilians are rationed, conscripted and targeted, and propaganda shapes opinion, as in both world wars.",
        """(?:the )?war guilt clause|article 231""" to "Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles, which made Germany accept responsibility for causing the First World War and so for paying reparations. Germans bitterly resented it.",
        """(?:the )?paris peace conference(?: of 1919)?""" to "The 1919 meeting of the war's winners in Paris, led by the Big Four (Wilson, Lloyd George, Clemenceau and Orlando), that wrote the peace treaties, above all the Treaty of Versailles with Germany, and founded the League of Nations. The losers were not invited to negotiate.",
        """(?:the )?february revolution""" to "The March 1917 uprising in Petrograd (February in Russia's old calendar) of striking workers and mutinous soldiers that forced Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate and set up a Provisional Government, which the Bolsheviks overthrew in October.",
        """(?:leon )?trotsky""" to "A Bolshevik leader who organized the October Revolution and built the Red Army in the civil war. He lost the power struggle to Stalin after Lenin's death, was exiled in 1929, and was murdered on Stalin's orders in Mexico in 1940.",
        """war communism""" to "The Bolsheviks' economic policy during the Russian Civil War, 1918 to 1921: industry was nationalized, private trade banned and grain seized from peasants to feed the Red Army and cities. It caused famine and revolt and was replaced by the New Economic Policy.",
        """(?:forced )?collectivi[sz]ation(?: of agriculture)?""" to "Stalin's policy from 1929 of forcing peasants to give up their land and animals and join large state-run collective farms. Resisting kulaks were shot or deported, and the chaos led to famines that killed millions, including the Holodomor in Ukraine.",
        """(?:the )?march on rome""" to "The October 1922 march of Mussolini's Blackshirts on Rome, after which King Victor Emmanuel III made Mussolini prime minister, bringing the Fascists to power in Italy.",
        """(?:the )?(?:beer hall|munich) putsch""" to "Hitler's failed attempt to seize power in Munich in November 1923. He was jailed for it and wrote Mein Kampf in prison.",
        """(?:the )?enabling act(?: of 1933)?""" to "The March 1933 law that let Hitler's cabinet make laws without the Reichstag for four years, turning his government into a legal dictatorship.",
        """(?:the )?(?:remilitari[sz]ation of the rhineland|rhineland remilitari[sz]ation|reoccupation of the rhineland)""" to "In March 1936 Hitler sent troops into the Rhineland, the German land by the French border that the Treaty of Versailles had made a demilitarized zone. Britain and France did nothing.",
        """(?:the )?anschluss""" to "Nazi Germany's annexation of Austria in March 1938, which the Treaty of Versailles had forbidden.",
        """(?:the )?(?:german )?invasion of poland""" to "Germany invaded Poland on September 1, 1939, and the Soviet Union from the east on September 17. Britain and France declared war on Germany on September 3, beginning the Second World War in Europe.",
        """(?:the )?battle of britain""" to "The air battle over Britain from July to October 1940, when the Royal Air Force beat off the Luftwaffe, so Hitler called off his planned invasion. Churchill: \"Never was so much owed by so many to so few.\"",
        """(?:the )?battle of midway""" to "The June 1942 naval battle in the Pacific in which the United States sank four Japanese aircraft carriers, the turning point of the Pacific war.",
        """(?:the )?potsdam conference""" to "The July and August 1945 meeting of Truman, Stalin and Churchill (then Attlee) near Berlin that split Germany into four occupation zones, set its new borders and demanded Japan's unconditional surrender.",
        """(?:the )?(?:us |american )?(?:policy of containment|containment policy)|containment (?:in|during) the cold war""" to "The US Cold War policy, set out by George Kennan in 1947, of stopping communism from spreading to new countries, through the Truman Doctrine, the Marshall Plan, NATO and the Korean and Vietnam wars.",
        """(?:the )?long march""" to "The 1934 to 1935 retreat of China's Communists from the Nationalists, about 10,000 km across China. Only about a tenth of the marchers survived, and Mao Zedong emerged as their leader.",
        """(?:the )?tiananmen square (?:protests?|massacre)(?: of 1989)?|(?:the )?1989 tiananmen square protests?""" to "The spring 1989 protests in Beijing, led by students, calling for democracy and free speech, crushed by the army on June 3 and 4, 1989, killing hundreds or more.",
        """(?:the )?khmer rouge""" to "The communist movement led by Pol Pot that ruled Cambodia from 1975 to 1979, emptied the cities into forced labour camps and killed about 1.5 to 2 million people, about a quarter of the population, in the Cambodian genocide.",
        """perestroika""" to "\"Restructuring\": Mikhail Gorbachev's reforms from 1986 to revive the Soviet economy by loosening central planning and allowing some private business.",
        """(?:the )?bolsheviks?""" to "The radical wing of Russia's Marxist party, led by Vladimir Lenin, which seized power in the October Revolution of 1917, won the civil war and became the Communist Party of the Soviet Union.",
        """(?:the )?mensheviks?""" to "The moderate wing of Russia's Marxist party, which split from the Bolsheviks in 1903 and lost out to them after 1917.",
        """(?:the )?new economic policy|(?:the )?nep""" to "Lenin's 1921 retreat from full state control after the civil war: peasants could sell surplus grain and small businesses could trade privately for profit, while the state kept heavy industry, banks and foreign trade. Stalin ended it in 1928.",
        """(?:stalin'?s )?five[- ]year plans?""" to "Stalin's state plans, starting in 1928, to industrialize the Soviet Union fast, with targets for coal, steel, oil and electricity, paired with the forced collectivization of farms. Output rose sharply at a huge human cost.",
        """(?:the )?weimar republic""" to "Germany's democratic republic from 1919 to 1933, named for the town where its constitution was written. It was weakened by the Treaty of Versailles, hyperinflation in 1923 and the Depression, and ended when Hitler took power.",
        """german hyperinflation|hyperinflation in germany|(?:the )?(?:german )?hyperinflation of 1923""" to "In 1923 German money became nearly worthless: the government printed money to pay reparations and striking workers after France occupied the Ruhr, and prices doubled every few days, until a loaf of bread cost billions of marks. Savings were wiped out.",
        """(?:the )?munich (?:agreement|pact|conference)""" to "The September 1938 deal in which Britain (Chamberlain) and France let Hitler take the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia in exchange for a promise of no more demands. It is the classic example of appeasement; Germany took the rest of Czechoslovakia in March 1939.",
        """appeasement""" to "The 1930s policy, led by British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain, of giving in to Hitler's demands to avoid another war, culminating in the Munich Agreement of 1938. It ended when Germany invaded Poland in 1939.",
        """(?:the )?(?:nazi[- ]soviet|molotov[- ]ribbentrop|german[- ]soviet) (?:non[- ]aggression )?pact""" to "The August 1939 non-aggression pact between Hitler's Germany and Stalin's Soviet Union, with a secret deal to divide Poland and Eastern Europe. It freed Hitler to invade Poland a week later.",
        """blitzkrieg""" to "\"Lightning war\": Germany's way of fighting early in the Second World War, using fast tanks, motorized infantry and dive-bombers together to break through and encircle the enemy before it could react.",
        """(?:operation )?barbarossa""" to "Germany's invasion of the Soviet Union on June 22, 1941, the largest invasion in history. It stalled outside Moscow in the winter of 1941 and opened the war's deadliest front.",
        """(?:the )?(?:attack on |bombing of )?pearl harbou?r(?: attack)?""" to "Japan's surprise attack on the US naval base at Pearl Harbor, Hawaii, on December 7, 1941, which brought the United States into the Second World War.",
        """(?:the )?truman doctrine""" to "President Truman's 1947 promise of US support for free peoples resisting communism, starting with aid to Greece and Turkey. It began the policy of containment.",
        """(?:the )?berlin (?:airlift|blockade)""" to "When the Soviets blockaded West Berlin's roads and railways in June 1948, the Western Allies flew in food and fuel for almost a year, over 275,000 flights, until Stalin lifted the blockade in May 1949.",
        """glasnost""" to "\"Openness\": Mikhail Gorbachev's policy from 1986 of allowing more free speech, a freer press and open criticism of the Soviet past and government.",
        """(?:the )?july crisis(?: of 1914)?""" to "The weeks after Franz Ferdinand's assassination in 1914: Austria-Hungary, backed by Germany's \"blank cheque\", sent Serbia an ultimatum and declared war on July 28; Russia mobilized, Germany declared war on Russia and France and invaded Belgium, and Britain joined on August 4.",
        """(?:the )?battle of verdun""" to "The longest battle of the First World War, February to December 1916, a German attack meant to \"bleed France white\" at the fortress city of Verdun. France held, but about 700,000 men on both sides were killed or wounded.",
        """(?:the )?(?:battle of )?passchendaele|(?:the )?third battle of ypres""" to "The British offensive near Ypres, Belgium, from July to November 1917, fought in deep mud. Canadian troops under Arthur Currie took the ridge and village of Passchendaele in November, with about 16,000 Canadian casualties.",
        """(?:the )?(?:gallipoli|dardanelles) campaign|(?:the )?battle of gallipoli|gallipoli""" to "The failed 1915 Allied attempt, pushed by Churchill, to force the Dardanelles and knock the Ottoman Empire out of the war. Landings by British, French, Australian and New Zealand (ANZAC) troops stalled, and they withdrew in January 1916.",
        """(?:german )?unrestricted submarine warfare""" to "Germany's policy of sinking any ship, even neutral merchant ships, in the waters around Britain without warning, using U-boats. The sinking of the Lusitania in 1915 and its return in 1917 helped bring the United States into the First World War.",
        """(?:the )?zimmermann (?:telegram|note)""" to "A secret January 1917 German message, intercepted by Britain, proposing that Mexico join Germany against the United States in return for Texas, New Mexico and Arizona. It helped push the US into the war in April 1917.",
        """(?:the )?armistice(?: of (?:1918|compi(?:e|è)gne))?""" to "The ceasefire that ended the fighting of the First World War at 11 am on November 11, 1918, signed by Germany and the Allies in a railway carriage at Compiègne, France. It is marked as Remembrance Day.",
        """(?:war )?reparations""" to "Payments a defeated country makes for war damage. The Treaty of Versailles made Germany pay them, set in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks, which fed German resentment and the economic crisis of 1923.",
        """(?:the )?october revolution|(?:the )?bolshevik revolution""" to "The Bolshevik seizure of power in Petrograd led by Lenin and Trotsky on October 25, 1917 (November 7 in the modern calendar), overthrowing the Provisional Government.",
        """(?:tsar )?nicholas ii""" to "The last tsar of Russia, from 1894 to 1917. Defeats in the First World War and shortages at home led to his abdication in the February Revolution, and the Bolsheviks shot him and his family in 1918.",
        """(?:the )?kulaks?""" to "Better-off peasants in Russia who owned land and hired labour. Stalin called them class enemies during collectivization, and millions were shot, deported to the gulag or left to starve.",
        """(?:the )?gulags?""" to "The Soviet system of forced-labour camps, mostly in Siberia and the far north, where millions of prisoners, many of them political, worked in brutal conditions under Stalin.",
        """(?:the )?reichstag fire""" to "The burning of the German parliament building in February 1933, blamed on a Dutch communist. Hitler used it to suspend civil rights by emergency decree and arrest communists, weeks before the Enabling Act.",
        """(?:the )?night of the long knives""" to "The June 30, 1934 purge in which Hitler had the SS kill Ernst Röhm and other leaders of the SA (stormtroopers) along with political rivals, securing the army's support.",
        """(?:the )?abyssinian crisis|(?:the )?(?:italian )?invasion of (?:abyssinia|ethiopia)""" to "Mussolini's 1935 invasion of Abyssinia (Ethiopia). The League of Nations' weak sanctions failed to stop it, showing the League could not stop aggression by a major power.",
        """(?:the )?(?:rape of nanking|nanjing massacre|nanking massacre)""" to "The mass killing and rape of Chinese civilians and prisoners by Japanese troops after they took Nanjing (Nanking) in December 1937; estimates of the dead run up to 300,000.",
        """(?:the )?(?:evacuation of )?dunkirk(?: evacuation)?|operation dynamo""" to "The May and June 1940 rescue of about 338,000 trapped British and French soldiers from the beaches of Dunkirk, France, by warships and hundreds of small civilian boats.",
        """vichy (?:france|regime|government)""" to "The French government from 1940 to 1944, based in Vichy and led by Marshal Pétain, which collaborated with Nazi Germany after France's defeat, including in deporting Jews.",
        """(?:the )?final solution""" to "The Nazi plan, set out at the Wannsee Conference in January 1942, to murder all the Jews of Europe, carried out in death camps such as Auschwitz and Treblinka.",
        """auschwitz(?:-birkenau)?""" to "The largest Nazi concentration and extermination camp, in occupied Poland, where about 1.1 million people, about a million of them Jews, were murdered. Soviet troops freed it on January 27, 1945.",
        """(?:the )?battle of the bulge|(?:the )?ardennes offensive""" to "Germany's last major offensive on the Western Front, a surprise attack through the Ardennes in December 1944 that made a \"bulge\" in the American lines before it was pushed back by January 1945.",
        """(?:the )?domino theory""" to "The Cold War belief, stated by Eisenhower in 1954, that if one country fell to communism its neighbours would fall one after another like dominoes. It was used to justify the Vietnam War.",
        """mutually? assured destruction""" to "The Cold War idea that neither superpower would start a nuclear war because both would be destroyed: each side kept enough weapons to strike back after an attack.",
        """d(?:e|é)tente""" to "The easing of Cold War tensions between the superpowers in the late 1960s and 1970s, with arms control (SALT I in 1972), trade and the Helsinki Accords of 1975. It ended with the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan in 1979.",
        """(?:the )?tet offensive""" to "The surprise attack by North Vietnamese and Viet Cong forces on cities across South Vietnam in January 1968. It failed militarily but turned American opinion against the Vietnam War.",
        """(?:the )?soviet(?:-afghan war| invasion of afghanistan| war in afghanistan)""" to "The Soviet Union invaded Afghanistan in December 1979 to prop up its communist government and fought US-backed mujahideen guerrillas until it withdrew in 1989, a costly defeat that helped weaken the USSR.",
        """(?:the )?solidarity (?:movement|trade union|union)(?: in poland)?|(?:the )?solidarity in poland|(?:the )?polish solidarity(?: movement)?|solidarno(?:s|ś)(?:c|ć)""" to "The independent trade union founded in 1980 at the Gdańsk shipyard in Poland, led by Lech Wałęsa. It grew into a mass movement against communist rule and won Poland's 1989 election.",
        """(?:the )?people'?s republic of china|(?:the )?prc""" to "The communist state founded by Mao Zedong on October 1, 1949, after the Communists won the Chinese Civil War and the Nationalists fled to Taiwan.",
        """(?:the )?hundred flowers (?:campaign|movement)""" to "Mao's 1956 to 1957 invitation for intellectuals to criticize the Communist Party (\"let a hundred flowers bloom\"). When criticism poured in, it was followed by the Anti-Rightist Campaign, which punished hundreds of thousands of critics.",
        """(?:the )?salt march|(?:the )?dandi march|(?:the )?salt satyagraha""" to "Gandhi's 1930 walk of about 390 km to the sea at Dandi to make salt in defiance of the British salt tax, a famous act of non-violent civil disobedience against British rule.",
        """(?:the )?(?:fall|collapse) of communism(?: in eastern europe)?|(?:the )?revolutions of 1989""" to "In 1989 communist governments across Eastern Europe fell, mostly peacefully: Poland's Solidarity won elections, Hungary opened its border, the Berlin Wall fell on November 9, and Czechoslovakia's Velvet Revolution and Romania's revolt followed. Gorbachev did not send in troops.",
        """trench warfare""" to "Fighting from long lines of dug-in trenches, protected by barbed wire and machine guns, that marked the Western Front of the First World War from late 1914 to 1918. Attacks across no man's land cost huge numbers of lives for little ground.",
        """(?:the )?treaty of versailles""" to "The 1919 peace treaty between the Allies and Germany that ended the First World War. Germany lost territory and its colonies, had its army cut to 100,000 men, had to accept blame for the war (the war guilt clause) and pay reparations. Its harsh terms fed German resentment that Hitler exploited.",
        """(?:the )?league of nations""" to "The international organization set up in 1920 by the Paris Peace Conference, Woodrow Wilson's idea, to keep the peace through collective security. The United States never joined, and it failed to stop Japan, Italy and Germany in the 1930s. The United Nations replaced it in 1945.",
        """(?:vladimir )?lenin""" to "The leader of the Bolsheviks, who led the October Revolution of 1917, founded the Soviet state, won the civil war and brought in the New Economic Policy. He died in 1924.",
        """(?:joseph )?stalin""" to "The dictator of the Soviet Union from the late 1920s until his death in 1953. He forced through five-year plans and collectivization, killed or imprisoned millions in the purges and the gulag, led the USSR to victory in the Second World War, and took control of Eastern Europe.",
        """(?:the )?holodomor""" to "The man-made famine in Soviet Ukraine in 1932 and 1933, caused by Stalin's collectivization and grain seizures, that killed about 3.5 to 5 million Ukrainians. Canada recognizes it as a genocide.",
        """fascism""" to "A far-right, authoritarian movement of extreme nationalism under a single all-powerful leader, with one party, no free press or opposition, and glorification of the state and war. Mussolini founded it in Italy in 1919; Nazism was the German form.",
        """(?:benito )?mussolini""" to "The founder of fascism, who ruled Italy as dictator (Il Duce) from 1922, invaded Ethiopia in 1935 and allied with Hitler. He was overthrown in 1943 and shot by Italian partisans in 1945.",
        """(?:the )?battle of stalingrad""" to "The battle for the Soviet city of Stalingrad from August 1942 to February 1943, which ended with a German army of about 300,000 surrounded and destroyed. It was the turning point on the Eastern Front, with nearly two million casualties.",
        """(?:the )?manhattan project""" to "The secret American project, with British and Canadian help, that built the first atomic bombs, led by J. Robert Oppenheimer at Los Alamos. The first test was in July 1945, and the bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.",
        """(?:the )?yalta conference""" to "The February 1945 meeting of Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin in Crimea that planned the defeat and division of Germany, agreed on free elections in Eastern Europe (which Stalin did not keep) and on founding the United Nations.",
        """(?:the )?warsaw pact""" to "The military alliance of the Soviet Union and its Eastern European satellite states, formed in 1955 as an answer to NATO. It was used to crush the Prague Spring in 1968 and dissolved in 1991.",
        """mao(?: zedong| tse-tung)?|chairman mao""" to "The leader of the Chinese Communist Party who won the civil war and founded the People's Republic of China in 1949, then ruled it until his death in 1976. His Great Leap Forward and Cultural Revolution caused tens of millions of deaths.",
        """(?:the )?great leap forward""" to "Mao's 1958 to 1962 campaign to industrialize China fast through huge communes and backyard steel furnaces. Farming collapsed and the famine that followed killed an estimated 15 to 45 million people.",
        """(?:the )?(?:great proletarian )?cultural revolution""" to "Mao's 1966 to 1976 campaign to purge China of \"capitalist\" and traditional elements. Young Red Guards attacked teachers, officials and temples, millions were persecuted and between hundreds of thousands and millions died.",
        """(?:mahatma |mohandas )?gandhi""" to "The leader of India's independence movement against British rule, who used non-violent civil disobedience such as the Salt March of 1930. India won independence in 1947, and he was assassinated in 1948.",
        """decoloni[sz]ation""" to "The process by which colonies won independence from European empires, above all after 1945: India and Pakistan in 1947, most of Africa from the late 1950s to the 1970s, some peacefully and some through wars such as in Algeria and Vietnam.",
        """apartheid""" to "South Africa's system of legal racial segregation and white minority rule from 1948 to the early 1990s. Black South Africans were denied the vote and forced into separate areas. It ended with the 1994 election that made Nelson Mandela president.",
        """(?:nelson )?mandela""" to "The anti-apartheid leader of the African National Congress who spent 27 years in prison, was freed in 1990, shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993 and became South Africa's first Black president in 1994.",
        """(?:the )?(?:rwandan|rwanda) genocide|(?:the )?genocide in rwanda|(?:the )?genocide against the tutsi""" to "The 1994 mass killing in Rwanda in which Hutu extremists murdered about 800,000 Tutsi and moderate Hutu in about 100 days. Canadian general Roméo Dallaire led the small UN force that the world refused to reinforce.",
        """(?:the )?russian civil war""" to "The 1917 to 1922 war after the October Revolution between the Bolsheviks' Red Army, led by Trotsky, and the anti-communist Whites, who had foreign help. The Reds won and founded the Soviet Union in 1922.",
        """(?:the )?great (?:purge|terror)|(?:stalin'?s )?purges""" to "Stalin's campaign of terror from 1936 to 1938: show trials of old Bolsheviks, purges of the army and party, and about 700,000 executions, with more sent to the gulag.",
        """totalitarianism""" to "A system in which one party and leader control every part of life, the government, economy, media, education and even private thought, through propaganda, secret police and terror, as in Stalin's Soviet Union and Hitler's Germany.",
        """(?:the )?(?:atomic )?bombings? of hiroshima(?: and nagasaki)?|hiroshima""" to "The Japanese city destroyed by the first atomic bomb used in war, dropped by the United States on August 6, 1945, killing about 70,000 to 140,000 people. A second bomb hit Nagasaki on August 9, and Japan surrendered on August 15.",
        """(?:the )?berlin wall""" to "The wall East Germany built in 1961 to stop its people fleeing to West Berlin, the symbol of the Cold War's divided Europe. It fell on November 9, 1989.",
        """(?:the )?chinese civil war""" to "The war between Chiang Kai-shek's Nationalists (the Kuomintang) and Mao Zedong's Communists, from 1927 to 1949 with a pause to fight Japan. The Communists won in 1949, and the Nationalists fled to Taiwan.",
        """chiang kai[- ]shek""" to "The leader of China's Nationalists (the Kuomintang) who fought the Communists and Japan, lost the civil war in 1949 and ruled the Republic of China on Taiwan until his death in 1975.",
        """(?:the )?partition of india""" to "The 1947 division of British India into Hindu-majority India and Muslim-majority Pakistan (with East Pakistan, later Bangladesh). About 15 million people were uprooted and up to a million or more died in the violence.",
        """(?:the )?universal declaration of human rights|(?:the )?udhr""" to "The statement of basic rights and freedoms for everyone, adopted by the United Nations on December 10, 1948. Canadian John Humphrey wrote its first draft, and Eleanor Roosevelt led the committee.",
        """(?:dr.? )?martin luther king(?: jr.?)?|mlk""" to "The American Baptist minister who led the civil rights movement with non-violent protest, from the Montgomery bus boycott in 1955 to the March on Washington in 1963 and his \"I Have a Dream\" speech. He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964 and was assassinated in 1968.",
        """(?:mikhail )?gorbachev""" to "The last leader of the Soviet Union, from 1985 to 1991, whose reforms of glasnost and perestroika, and refusal to use force in Eastern Europe in 1989, helped end the Cold War. He won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1990.",
        """(?:the )?algerian war(?: of independence)?""" to "Algeria's 1954 to 1962 war of independence against France, fought by the National Liberation Front (FLN), with brutality and torture on both sides. Algeria became independent in 1962.",
        """(?:the )?scramble for africa""" to "The rush by European powers to seize and divide almost all of Africa between about 1881 and 1914, with rules set at the Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885. By 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia were independent.",
        """(?:the )?treaty of brest[- ]litovsk""" to "The March 1918 treaty in which Lenin's Russia left the First World War, giving up Poland, the Baltic states, Ukraine and Finland to Germany and its allies.",
        """(?:the )?spanish flu|(?:the )?1918 (?:flu|influenza)(?: pandemic)?""" to "The influenza pandemic of 1918 to 1920, which killed about 50 million people worldwide, more than the First World War.",
        """(?:the )?armenian genocide""" to "The killing of about 1.5 million Armenians by the Ottoman government from 1915, through massacres and death marches into the Syrian desert.",
        """(?:the )?spanish civil war""" to "The 1936 to 1939 war between Spain's elected Republican government and Franco's Nationalists, who were backed by Hitler and Mussolini. Franco won and ruled as dictator until 1975.",
        """(?:the )?nuremberg laws""" to "The 1935 Nazi laws that stripped German Jews of citizenship and banned marriage between Jews and other Germans.",
        """kristallnacht|(?:the )?night of broken glass""" to "The pogrom of November 9 and 10, 1938, when Nazis burned synagogues, smashed Jewish shops and homes, killed about 90 Jews and sent 30,000 Jewish men to concentration camps.",
        """(?:the )?nuremberg trials""" to "The 1945 to 1946 trials of leading Nazis by the Allies for war crimes, crimes against peace and crimes against humanity, which set the principle that following orders is no defence.",
        """(?:the )?suez crisis""" to "In 1956 Britain, France and Israel invaded Egypt after Nasser nationalized the Suez Canal; US and Soviet pressure forced them out. Canada's Lester Pearson won the Nobel Peace Prize for proposing the first UN peacekeeping force to end it.",
        """(?:the )?cuban revolution""" to "The 1953 to 1959 revolt in which Fidel Castro and Che Guevara overthrew the dictator Batista and set up a communist state allied with the Soviet Union.",
        """(?:the )?bay of pigs(?: invasion)?""" to "The failed April 1961 invasion of Cuba by CIA-trained Cuban exiles trying to overthrow Castro, which pushed Cuba closer to the Soviet Union.",
        """(?:the )?space race""" to "The Cold War contest between the Soviet Union and the United States in spaceflight, from Sputnik in 1957 and Gagarin in 1961 to the American Moon landing in 1969.",
        """sputnik(?: 1)?""" to "The first artificial satellite, launched by the Soviet Union on October 4, 1957, which started the space race.",
        """(?:the )?hungarian revolution(?: of 1956)?""" to "The 1956 uprising in Hungary against Soviet control, crushed by Soviet tanks within weeks.",
        """(?:the )?prague spring""" to "Czechoslovakia's 1968 reforms under Alexander Dubček toward \"socialism with a human face\", ended by a Warsaw Pact invasion in August 1968.",
        """(?:the )?iranian revolution|(?:the )?(?:1979 )?islamic revolution""" to "The 1979 revolution in which the Shah of Iran was overthrown and Ayatollah Khomeini set up an Islamic republic.",
        """(?:the )?srebrenica (?:massacre|genocide)""" to "The July 1995 killing of about 8,000 Bosniak Muslim men and boys by Bosnian Serb forces at Srebrenica, during the Bosnian War.",
        """ho chi minh""" to "The Vietnamese communist leader who led the fight for independence from France, declared Vietnam independent in 1945 and led North Vietnam until his death in 1969.",
    ).map { Triple(Regex("^(?:" + it.first + ")$"), it.second, it.first) }

    private fun out(body: String) = Answer.Text(heading = null, body = body, source = "Nimble history")

    fun answer(q: String): Answer? {
        for ((re, body) in facts) if (re.containsMatchIn(q)) return out(body)
        val t = q.replace(Regex("^(?:who|what) (?:was|were|is|are) "), "").replace(Regex(" in (?:history|world history)$"), "")
        return Terms.answer(t, terms, emptyList())?.let { out(it) }
    }
}
